A 4KB buffer in a function that calls itself.

vchroot_run resolves symlinks BY CALLING ITSELF, up to MAX_SYMLINK_DEPTH of 10, and it held char
dirents[4096] in its frame. Measured from the built dylib, the frame was over 0x1038 bytes; it is
now 0x2e8. Five and a half times less stack per level, on the function every single path
translation in the guest goes through.

The buffer moves into struct context, which the caller allocates once. Safe to share across the
recursion, which is the only thing that had to be checked: the getdents scan that uses it finishes
at done_getdents, the symlink branch that recurses comes after, and nothing reads it once the scan
is done.

WHY IT WAS LOOKED AT. Money Manager EX segfaulted when its Currency Manager panel was built, and the
core named the instruction:

  _vchroot_run+106:  mov qword ptr [rbp - 0x1038], rax

a write into its own frame, which only faults on a guard page. The chain into it is entirely
ordinary: the application calls wcsftime, which calls tzset, which faccessats the timezone file,
which goes through this translation.

WHAT IT DID AND DID NOT DO, measured rather than hoped. The vchroot fault site is GONE from the
core, and the application now gets further. It still does not open the Currency Manager: it now
crashes LATER and somewhere else, at objc_msgSend+29, which is a message to an object that is not
there. So this is a real reduction in a real risk and it is NOT the fix for that scenario. Task #243
carries what is left.

Gates: run-dts-batch, buck-bundled-patch-record-check, roster-sweep and roster-input.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/vchroot_userspace.c
